      "text": "To find the value of the fourth term of the sequence \\(3^0, 3^0 + 3^1, 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2, \\ldots\\), we start by identifying the general formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence.\n\nThe sequence can be expressed as the sum of consecutive powers of 3:\n\\[\na_n = 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + \\cdots + 3^n\n\\]\nThis is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = 3^0 = 1\\) and common ratio \\(r = 3\\). The sum of the first \\(n+1\\) terms of a geometric series is given by:\n\\[\nS_{n+1} = \\frac{a(r^{n+1} - 1)}{r - 1}\n\\]\nIn our case, \\(a = 1\\), \\(r = 3\\), and the number of terms is \\(n+1\\), so the fourth term corresponds to \\(n = 3\\) (since the sequence starts from \\(n = 0\\), so \\(n+1 = 4\\)). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\na_4 = \\frac{1(3^4 - 1)}{3 - 1} = \\frac{3^4 - 1}{2} = \\frac{81 - 1}{2} = \\frac{80}{2} = 40\n\\]\nThus, the simplified value of the fourth term of the sequence is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{40}\n\\]",
